1
00:00:00,269 --> 00:00:04,930
Let's cut to the chase: How does the Higgs
field "give" particles mass? (and to be clear,

2
00:00:04,930 --> 00:00:08,590
we're talking about the Higgs field and NOT
the Higgs Boson, which is merely an excitation

3
00:00:08,590 --> 00:00:11,119
leftover after the process we're about to
explain.

4
00:00:11,119 --> 00:00:13,219
But I digress…back to mass!)

5
00:00:13,219 --> 00:00:17,030
To begin, we need to know what we even mean
by "mass" - so we'll head the other direction

6
00:00:17,030 --> 00:00:20,900
and talk about what it means to be massless:
This may sound crazy, but the defining feature

7
00:00:20,900 --> 00:00:24,050
of any particle without mass is that it travels
at the speed of light.

8
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:28,140
In fact, if we're honest it should really
be called the "speed of massless particles,"

9
00:00:28,140 --> 00:00:31,770
but since the first massless particles we
knew about were photons of light, the name

10
00:00:31,770 --> 00:00:32,770
has stuck.

11
00:00:32,770 --> 00:00:37,370
Anyway, the point is that all massless particles
travel 300 million meters every second.

12
00:00:37,370 --> 00:00:41,120
The details of this are explained by special
relativity, but simply put, it's physically

13
00:00:41,120 --> 00:00:44,870
impossible for a massless particle to NOT
travel at 300 million meters per second.

14
00:00:44,870 --> 00:00:45,870
They can travel in a straight line or bounce
off of things and change direction, but the

15
00:00:45,870 --> 00:00:46,870
speed of a massless particle never changes.

16
00:00:46,870 --> 00:00:49,329
And so mass is just the property of NOT HAVING
TO always travel at the speed of light.

17
00:00:49,329 --> 00:00:53,450
As a side effect, this also means not being
ABLE to travel at the speed of light, but

18
00:00:53,450 --> 00:00:56,700
the key is that particles with mass are lucky
enough that they get to travel at ANY speed

19
00:00:56,700 --> 00:00:59,149
they want – as long as it's slower than
light.

20
00:00:59,149 --> 00:01:02,560
The amount of mass something has just tells
how hard it is for it to change from one of

21
00:01:02,560 --> 00:01:03,969
these speeds to another.

22
00:01:03,969 --> 00:01:07,490
Now, in Part I we mentioned that if there
were no Higgs field in the Standard Model,

23
00:01:07,490 --> 00:01:10,399
ALL particles should be massless and thus
travel at the speed of light.

24
00:01:10,399 --> 00:01:14,130
But you and I and swiss cheese clearly have
mass, because we have the beautiful luxury

25
00:01:14,130 --> 00:01:16,219
of being able to sit still.

26
00:01:16,219 --> 00:01:18,170
So how does the Higgs field help us do that?

27
00:01:18,170 --> 00:01:22,219
Well, while massless particles can only travel
at the speed of light, they ARE allowed to

28
00:01:22,219 --> 00:01:23,219
bounce off of things.

29
00:01:23,219 --> 00:01:27,490
Things like particles, which are really just
excitations in a quantum field.

30
00:01:27,490 --> 00:01:32,119
For example, the electron field is more concentrated
at certain places called "electrons" - and

31
00:01:32,119 --> 00:01:34,349
everywhere else is "empty space".

32
00:01:34,349 --> 00:01:38,219
But the Higgs field is unusual in that it
has a high value EVERYWHERE – and to be

33
00:01:38,219 --> 00:01:42,700
clear, this high value is NOT the famous Higgs
Boson - that's an extra excitation in addition

34
00:01:42,700 --> 00:01:44,680
to this already elevated field.

35
00:01:44,680 --> 00:01:48,929
But because the Higgs field has this everywhere
non-zero value, any particle that CAN interact

36
00:01:48,929 --> 00:01:51,429
with it is pretty much bouncing off of it
all the time.

37
00:01:51,429 --> 00:01:55,670
And if a massless particle bounces back and
forth and back and forth (or, since it's quantum

38
00:01:55,670 --> 00:01:59,689
mechanics, does both at the same time), then
even though in between bounces it travels

39
00:01:59,689 --> 00:02:03,450
at the speed of light, when you add everything
up it LOOKS like the particle is going slower

40
00:02:03,450 --> 00:02:04,469
than light.

41
00:02:04,469 --> 00:02:05,469
Even...

42
00:02:05,469 --> 00:02:06,469
like it's not moving!

43
00:02:06,469 --> 00:02:10,580
And since only things with mass are allowed
to not move, our massless particle now looks

44
00:02:10,580 --> 00:02:12,440
and acts like it has mass.

45
00:02:12,440 --> 00:02:14,010
Well done, Higgs!

46
00:02:14,010 --> 00:02:17,870
What's more, the Higgs field can even interact
with its own excitations, which is to say,

47
00:02:17,870 --> 00:02:20,450
it can give mass to the Higgs Boson, too.

48
00:02:20,450 --> 00:02:23,890
Actually, the Higgs field likes to interact
with itself so much more than with the lowly

49
00:02:23,890 --> 00:02:27,690
electrons and protons that make us up, that
the Higgs Boson has a great deal more mass

50
00:02:27,690 --> 00:02:28,690
– and that's part of why it's been so hard
to find.

51
00:02:28,690 --> 00:02:31,490
But we shouldn't complain, because even though
the Higgs has given us a lot of trouble and

52
00:02:31,490 --> 00:02:35,570
only a little bit of mass, at least we have
mass, which allows us the simple pleasure

53
00:02:35,570 --> 00:02:36,370
of not moving.
